<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.3.4: Match Expressions and Tables with Situations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01282</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.3.4: Match Expressions and Tables with Situations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>107269f0-8542-4b95-86a7-2f9d5dcb3dc5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">Here are verbal descriptions of two situations, followed by tables and expressions that could help answer one of the
  questions in the situations.</para>
<list id="list-00001">
  <item> Situation 1: A person has 80 followers on social media. The number of followers triples each year. How many
    followers will she have after 4 years? </item>
  <item> Situation 2: A tank contains 80 gallons of water and is getting filled at a rate of 3 gallons per minute. How
    many gallons of water will be in the tank after 4 minutes? </item>
</list>
<para id="para-00002">Use the descriptions of Situation 1 and Situation 2 to help you answer questions 1 – 6. Be prepared to explain
  how the table or expression answers the question.</para>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002"><title>Activity</title><exercise id="b3abcd5f-9a20-45b8-9a89-cd48fa8ad671" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00003">Which situation best describes \(80 \cdot 3 \cdot 3 \cdot 3 \cdot 3\)? </para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00004">Correct! Situation 1 </para><para id="para-00005">Incorrect. The correct answer is Situation 1.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="db6e2c86-ad77-4d31-b026-d09c343446fa" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00006"> Which situation best describes the following table? </para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
            <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
              0
            </entry>
            <entry>
              1
            </entry>
            <entry>
              2
            </entry>
            <entry>
              3
            </entry>
            <entry>
              4
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
              80
            </entry>
            <entry>
              240
            </entry>
            <entry>
              720
            </entry>
            <entry>
              2,160
            </entry>
            <entry>
              6,480
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      
    </problem><solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00007">Correct! Situation 1. The table shows the growth of the followers multiplied by 3 each year. </para><para id="para-00008">Incorrect. The correct answer is Situation 1. The table shows the growth of the followers multiplied by 3 each
        year.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_3d437c94-a607-45ea-87b2-d75018374847" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00003"><para id="para-00009">Which situation best describes \(80+3+3+3+3\)? </para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00003"><para id="para-00010">Correct! Situation 2 </para><para id="para-00011">Incorrect. The correct answer is Situation 2.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_6664cb81-3aa9-44bb-b4c6-2095d8377810" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00012"> Which situation best describes \(80+4 \cdot 3\)? </para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00013">Correct! Situation 2. \(4 \cdot 3\) is another way of expressing \(3+3+3+3\).</para><para id="para-00014">Incorrect. The correct answer is Situation 2. \(4 \cdot 3\) is another way of expressing \(3+3+3+3\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="f10b1fd5-6fb0-4c9d-a603-9085bcf49d55" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00005"><para id="para-00015"> Which situation best describes the following table? </para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00002">
        <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
            <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
              0
            </entry>
            <entry>
              1
            </entry>
            <entry>
              2
            </entry>
            <entry>
              3
            </entry>
            <entry>
              4
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
              80
            </entry>
            <entry>
              83
            </entry>
            <entry>
              86
            </entry>
            <entry>
              89
            </entry>
            <entry>
              92
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      
    </problem><solution id="sol-00005"><para id="para-00016">Correct! Situation 2. The table shows the gallons of water in the tank increasing by 3 gallons each minute.
      </para><para id="para-00017">Incorrect. The correct answer is Situation 2. The table shows the gallons of water in the tank increasing by 3
        gallons each minute.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_446ac711-2e50-418b-9706-849368de01bc" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00006"><para id="para-00018"> Which situation best describes \(80\cdot 81\)? </para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00006"><para id="para-00019">Correct! Situation 1. The 81 comes from \(3 \cdot 3 \cdot 3 \cdot 3\).</para><para id="para-00020">Incorrect. The correct answer is Situation 1.The 81 comes from \(3 \cdot 3 \cdot 3 \cdot 3\) </para></solution></exercise></section>

<title>Video: Matching Verbal Situations to Expressions and Tables</title>
<para id="para-00021">Watch the following video to learn more about matching verbal representations of a function with tables and
  expressions.</para>
